1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,912
Welcome to Module Fourteen, and to the unit on food webs: systems with more than one agent.

2
00:00:05,912 --> 00:00:11,112
Ask most people how an ant colony works, and they'll tell you about a queen giving orders.

3
00:00:11,112 --> 00:00:12,400
You know that's wrong.

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,562
The queen mostly lays eggs.

5
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:17,425
The real coordination happens from the bottom up.

6
00:00:17,425 --> 00:00:24,962
A forager lays pheromone on a trail, others follow the strongest trail, and the trail itself becomes the message.

7
00:00:24,962 --> 00:00:28,687
Coordination through traces in a shared environment.

8
00:00:28,687 --> 00:00:29,762
Stigmergy.

9
00:00:29,762 --> 00:00:35,512
Both pictures, the boss and the trail, show up when people connect AI agents together.

10
00:00:35,512 --> 00:00:39,512
In the first, the manager pattern, one agent stays in charge.

11
00:00:39,512 --> 00:00:46,050
It calls specialist agents the way it would call a tool, collects their answers, and speaks to you with one voice.

12
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:50,637
In the second, the decentralised pattern, agents hand off.

13
00:00:50,637 --> 00:00:58,550
A triage agent sees that your question is about an order, passes the whole conversation to the orders specialist, and steps out of the way.

14
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:03,775
And many real systems add a shared whiteboard that every agent reads and writes.

15
00:01:03,775 --> 00:01:05,912
That is stigmergy by another name.

16
00:01:05,912 --> 00:01:11,087
Now the most important lesson of the day, which comes straight from OpenAI's guide.

17
00:01:11,087 --> 00:01:12,962
Start with one agent.

18
00:01:12,962 --> 00:01:17,875
Give it good tools and clear instructions, and only split it when it really struggles.

19
00:01:17,875 --> 00:01:26,600
Usually that's when its instructions have grown into a tangle of special cases, or when it keeps choosing the wrong tool because several tools look alike.

20
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:28,850
When you do split, you gain things.

21
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:31,512
Each sub-agent needs less context.

22
00:01:31,512 --> 00:01:34,375
Simple subtasks can go to cheaper models.

23
00:01:34,375 --> 00:01:37,225
Independent work can run in parallel.

24
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:39,087
But you also lose things.

25
00:01:39,087 --> 00:01:43,987
Information gets dropped at every handoff, and a fixed plan can't adapt.

26
00:01:43,987 --> 00:01:53,350
In one experiment from the CMU course, an agent that had to stick to its first plan succeeded about a quarter of the time, and letting it revise the plan helped a lot.

27
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:56,912
A good rule of thumb: the signal for a team is width.

28
00:01:56,912 --> 00:01:59,537
Thirty papers to compare, not one.

29
00:01:59,537 --> 00:02:02,187
Here's where the colony comparison fails.

30
00:02:02,187 --> 00:02:08,837
A colony's coordination was shaped by millions of years of selection, and it survives losing workers.

31
00:02:08,837 --> 00:02:11,812
Your topology was designed last Tuesday.

32
00:02:11,812 --> 00:02:16,250
Every extra agent is another bill and another place to break.

33
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:20,750
And pheromone evaporates on its own, so stale trails fade.

34
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:24,050
A shared whiteboard does not fade unless you make it.

35
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:29,275
In the lab, you will put three papers you know well into a folder and build a small manager.

36
00:02:29,275 --> 00:02:38,212
It sends each paper to its own summariser, running in parallel, checks their answers with ordinary code, and then asks for one synthesis.

37
00:02:38,212 --> 00:02:42,237
Then you'll run a single agent on the same papers and compare.

38
00:02:42,237 --> 00:02:45,625
Because you know these papers, you get to be the judge.

39
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:48,525
Pick your three papers, and let's build a colony.

